{
  "markdown_report": "At 20% progress, select the prespecified terminal-supervision allocation experiment with explicit provenance. Its interpretation depends on the corrected baseline’s loss reduction: allocation can alter objective weighting, gradient-estimator variance, or both. No supplied result establishes an HR@10 improvement or an optimal probability.\n\nThree starting insights guide the experiment. First, Electronics has a 5.71% negative-wins rate versus 2.07% for Movies_and_TV. These training margins distinguish discrimination regimes but cannot establish validation ranking quality. Second, appended-MASK user RankMe is approximately 24 in Electronics and Industrial_and_Scientific, versus item RankMe near 49. This motivates geometry monitoring without diagnosing collapse. Third, training allocation is null across categories. Diagnostic terminal coverage near 20% cannot recover branch probabilities; the definitions also flag earlier prefix-context confounding.\n\nThree literature insights constrain interpretation. BERT4Rec [1] already adds terminal-only examples and appends MASK at inference. Neither its single-mask ablation nor masking-density experiments establish an optimal terminal-example allocation. Loss semantics [2] and stratified estimation [4,5] separate sample allocation from expected gradient weight. Sampling research [3] separates sampled ranking from full-catalog behavior; sequence perturbations [6] provide model-dependent sensitivity evidence, not observed intent.\n\nThe framework separates three directions:\n\n| Direction | Controlled component | Remaining gap |\n|---|---|---|\n| Supervision alignment | Terminal versus random allocation [1] | Context and normalization confounding |\n| Optimization accounting | Actual loss coefficients and branch presence [2,4,5] | Coefficients do not identify gradient influence or Adam updates |\n| Behavioral sensitivity | Frozen history perturbations [6] | Sensitivity does not establish useful reasoning or current intent |\n\nThree atomic ideas follow. Scores are originality/potential/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n| Idea | Scores | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prespecified p=0.75 allocation with preserved context [1,2] | 2/5/4 | Small, auditable experiment | Established technique; allocation rationale remains untested |\n| Sparse branch-gradient accounting [2,4] | 3/6/5 | Measures local interaction and informs later allocation studies | Extra compute; conflict need not harm ranking |\n| Matched history perturbations [6] | 3/5/3 | Separates recent-item reliance from earlier-order sensitivity | Diagnostic only; cannot infer unresolved needs |\n\nChoose the first idea. Preserve baseline prefix/window generators, corruption, architecture, optimizer, reduction, and empty-branch handling. Change only example allocation where supported; preserve distinct paired-view generators if present. With separately averaged branch losses and positive counts, allocation changes estimator variance rather than expected raw gradient weights. Stochastic empty branches attenuate omitted contributions; pooled target means have different weighting. Equal expected gradients do not imply equal Adam trajectories.\n\nRecord branch IDs, example/target counts, loss sums, denominators, coefficients, presence, and skipped batches; persist summaries with the selected checkpoint. Train one BERT4Rec per category. Preserve appended-MASK inference, maximum-nonpadding readout, and unchanged host macro validation HR@10 selection. Optional frozen probes compare recent versus matched random deletion, full versus latest-preserving shuffles, and last-item-only inputs using training-prefix targets and fixed candidates. Report paired uncertainty and Jaccard@10; these probes establish sensitivity only. Recency dependence is not automatically a shortcut. Effectiveness remains untested.\n\nReferences: [1] BERT4Rec, arXiv:1904.06690 and official generator. [2] PyTorch CrossEntropyLoss documentation. [3] On the Reliability of Sampling Strategies, RecSys 2025. [4] Stratified allocation results, Statistics Canada. [5] Importance Sampling for Stochastic Optimization, ICML 2015. [6] Does It Look Sequential?, RecSys 2024.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Prespecify terminal-only supervision probability p=0.75 per example while preserving the corrected BERT4Rec baseline’s context generators, random corruption procedure, and exact loss reduction. Train one independent model per category and persist explicit allocation accounting. Interpret the intervention according to the implemented reduction and branch-presence policy rather than assuming that 75% terminal examples means 75% terminal objective weight.",
    "motivation": "Terminal-only masking structurally matches appended-MASK prediction, but BERT4Rec already includes this construction. Unavailable training allocation and documented context confounding prevent attributing earlier behavior to branch probability. At 20% progress, a small, prespecified intervention with faithful accounting remains preferable to adding architectural complexity. Under separately normalized branch losses, unequal allocation primarily changes gradient-estimator variance when both branches are present; it does not inherently strengthen terminal supervision. The supplied margins and geometry are diagnostic observations, not evidence of improved HR@10, and no measured branch-gradient variance justifies 0.75 as optimal. The simulator feedback motivates investigating recent-context use, but this experiment does not directly model compatibility, duplication, quantities, unresolved needs, or latent traits.",
    "implementation_notes": "Apply the intervention to the supplied corrected baseline rather than inventing its missing implementation. Preserve Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl routing; DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; preprocessing seed 0; training seed 42; single-GPU execution; at most 200 epochs; and 200 batches per epoch. Keep artifacts beneath the trial directory and all training, debugging, and diagnostic compute within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Preserve baseline user/prefix endpoint sampling, truncation, positional IDs, corruption rules, optimizer, loss coefficients, empty-branch policy, and checkpoint-selection rule. Document the actual baseline allocation, batch size, branch construction, optimizer settings, validation cadence, selection ties, and stopping policy before interpreting the comparison. Upstream BERT4Rec code cannot establish these local settings. If the baseline uses distinct paired terminal and reconstruction views, retain their respective context generators; do not claim a shared-window probability-only comparison unless that equivalence is established. Define whether the allocation unit is an original example or a generated view, and preserve total processed examples and optimizer steps; replacing paired views with fewer views introduces another intervention. If preserving these quantities requires additional construction changes, disclose them and avoid a probability-only attribution. For the proposed stochastic allocation, draw one seeded Bernoulli decision per allocation unit with p_terminal=0.75. Do not silently replace it with a fixed 3:1 allocation: guaranteed positive branch counts are a distinct future experiment. With separate branch means and fixed coefficients w_t, unbiased within-branch samples, and guaranteed positive counts, expected raw gradients retain their branch weights despite allocation changes. For random counts with zero contribution from an absent branch, its expected contribution is attenuated by P(N_t>0); for B independent draws this equals 1-(1-p_t)^B. Preserve this baseline behavior, record absences, and report its magnitude rather than adding inverse-presence correction or redistributing weights. Per-branch target means also weight examples according to their target counts; changing target counts or within-branch selection can change the estimand. Pooled target means have allocation- and target-count-dependent coefficients. Log explicit branch counts, valid-target counts, detached CE sums, exact denominators, coefficients, objective contributions, and empty/skipped counts. For class-weighted cross-entropy, use the implemented applicable-weight denominator rather than the raw target count. Record aggregate loss sums divided by aggregate denominators separately from averages of batch objectives. Coefficient mass and example shares are not gradient shares. Save summaries alongside the selected checkpoint and reload them for diagnosis so allocation does not become unavailable after state_dict loading; ensure summaries identify their training interval and selected epoch. Preserve the reserved MASK token and masked-target supervision without visible shifted-target leakage. At inference keep at most L-1 history items, append MASK, left-pad, and select the maximum nonpadding index, never count-1. Keep the predictor writer consistent with checkpoint architecture, item mapping, MASK ID, sequence length, and positional convention. Preserve sample_batch, ModelView.model as the actual nn.Module, item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader) returning metrics and metric_definitions, and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Diagnostic batches remain (None, sequence, positive, negative), with B x L tensors. Record explicit sampler provenance because random reconstruction may also supervise the terminal position; unmatched bookkeeping and missing data remain null. Use only supplied histories, timestamps, titles, and descriptions; unavailable ratings, reviews, categories, prices, stores, and simulator traits remain unavailable. Optional sparse gradient probes use the same forward graph and actual branch coefficients/denominators, autograd.grad without optimizer updates, separate embedding/encoder norms and cosines, and undefined cosines for zero norms; preserve model mode, dropout realization, and training RNG, and account for compute. Variance-optimal allocation would require gradient covariance estimates: under independent sampling and fixed positive counts, covariance trace is minimized by n_t proportional to |w_t| times sqrt(trace(Sigma_t)). This does not establish optimality for Adam, random target denominators, or the present unmeasured setting, and is not implemented in this trial. Optional frozen sensitivity probes use training-history prefix targets, identical fixed candidates, and repeated local-RNG perturbations: recent deletion versus count-matched random deletion, reversal, full shuffling, latest-item-preserving shuffling, and last-item-only context. Never shuffle targets, padding, or appended MASK; reconstruct inputs consistently. Report paired HR@10 differences and top-10 Jaccard with user-clustered uncertainty and sample counts; bootstrap users while retaining their paired prefixes and perturbations. Identity controls should give zero change, known-score controls should recover known ranks, and deliberate readout/MASK corruptions may check probe sensitivity. These are proposed diagnostics rather than findings, and sensitivity or invariance alone establishes neither causal intent nor shortcut learning. Keep full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for twenty training users, five per category, separate from pinned sampled-candidate selection. Host selection remains unchanged four-category macro validation HR@10 on 200 validation users/category and 99 unique negative candidates with evaluation seed 42; do not modify candidates, splits, or evaluator, inspect hidden test data, fetch external data/weights, or repeatedly tune perturbations or allocation against the fixed validation users. No supplied validation scores support an effectiveness claim. Each category loads exactly one trained checkpoint, without averaging, independently fitted auxiliary predictors, or score fusion. Exact executable reproduction remains conditional on the absent baseline source and configuration; the pseudocode specifies the intervention and accounting contract.",
    "pseudocode": "for category in FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n    baseline = initialize_corrected_bert4rec(category, seed=42)\n    record_baseline_context_generators_and_loss_semantics(baseline)\n    record_actual_allocation_unit_batch_size_view_count_and_selection_rule()\n    # Preserve original context distributions, processed-example count, and steps.\n    # If paired views prevent this comparison, disclose the additional changes.\n    for epoch in baseline_epoch_schedule(max_epochs=200):\n        for step in range(200):\n            branches = baseline_seeded_branch_draws(p_terminal=0.75)\n            batch, provenance = baseline_construct_views(branches)\n            # Preserve each branch's original user/prefix/window generator.\n            # Terminal views replace their final nonpadding target with MASK.\n            # Random views retain original selection, replacement, and fallback.\n            outputs = baseline.model(batch.sequence)\n            loss, detached_accounting = exact_baseline_loss_with_accounting(\n                outputs, batch.labels, provenance\n            )\n            # Use original denominators, coefficients, and empty-branch handling.\n            # Separately normalized branches need not receive weights 0.75/0.25.\n            # Record branch presence and actual effective target coefficients.\n            baseline.optimizer_step(loss)\n            persist_training_counters(detached_accounting, provenance)\n        record_global_ratios_separately_from_mean_batch_objectives()\n        baseline_checkpoint_selection_using_unchanged_validation_rule()\n    save_one_selected_checkpoint_with_configuration_and_allocation_summary()\n    write_matching_category_predictor()\n\npredict(history, candidates):\n    sequence = baseline_left_pad(history[-(L-1):] + [MASK])\n    features = model.log2feats(sequence)\n    j = max_index_where(sequence != PAD)\n    return model.predict_score(features[j], candidates)\n\noptional_frozen_diagnosis(training_prefixes):\n    preserve_model_mode_and_training_rng()\n    for prefix, target, fixed_candidates in training_prefixes:\n        # Target is excluded from historical inputs and all permutations.\n        conditions = original_and_controlled_history_perturbations(prefix)\n        for history in conditions:\n            scores = predict(history, fixed_candidates)\n            record_target_rank_and_top10(scores)\n    report_paired_differences_jaccard_counts_and_user_clustered_intervals()\n    restore_model_mode_and_training_rng()\n\n# Host independently evaluates unchanged validation candidates and macro HR@10.\n# Compare with the supplied corrected baseline; no probability sweep or test access.\n# No executable baseline replacement is specified because its source is absent.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 2,
      "positive": "Explicitly separating allocation, context generation, target normalization, and branch presence strengthens the experiment’s interpretation.",
      "negative": "Terminal masking and altered example allocation are established techniques; p=0.75 introduces no new modeling principle."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Persisted provenance can support later controlled studies of context alignment and gradient-estimator variance within one BERT4Rec network.",
      "negative": "The allocation is not motivated by measured gradient variance and may leave the simulator’s specific relevance failures unresolved."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The intended change remains confined to training allocation and accounting, with no inference architecture change.",
      "negative": "Preserving paired-view semantics, example volume, target denominators, reproducibility, persisted diagnostics, and predictor consistency requires more work than changing a probability constant."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "BERT4Rec: Sequential Recommendation with Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformer",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690",
      "contributions": [
        "Establishes bidirectional masked-item learning and explicitly addresses its mismatch with next-item prediction by adding terminal-only examples.",
        "Appends a reserved MASK at inference and scores items from that token’s hidden representation.",
        "Provides the basis for preserving Cloze supervision without exposing visible shifted prediction targets."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its single-mask ablation does not isolate terminal-example allocation, and its masking-density experiments concern a different parameter.",
        "Does not establish p=0.75 as optimal for the supplied corrected baseline.",
        "Does not recover this trial’s missing sampler provenance or demonstrate its validation performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Official BERT4Rec training-data generator",
      "link": "https://github.com/FeiSun/BERT4Rec/blob/master/gen_data_fin.py",
      "contributions": [
        "Creates dupe_factor random-masked copies plus one explicitly terminal-only copy per sequence/window; dupe_factor=10 gives a 1/11 explicit terminal-example share, excluding incidental terminal targets.",
        "Distinguishes target-selection probability from MASK-replacement probability and uses complete short histories or deterministic sliding windows.",
        "Provides implementation context for documenting which baseline construction an allocation intervention changes."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The upstream example share is not a supervised-target share or objective coefficient.",
        "Upstream construction cannot establish the corrected local baseline’s sampler, masking probabilities, prefix distribution, or loss reduction.",
        "Evaluation construction using force_last must not be substituted for training context generation without disclosing the change."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch CrossEntropyLoss documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.CrossEntropyLoss.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Specifies ignored-target handling and mean-reduction denominators, including applicable class weights.",
        "Supports exact accounting of pooled versus separately normalized branch objectives."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Documents loss semantics rather than recommendation effectiveness.",
        "Loss coefficients and target counts alone do not determine gradient influence, optimizer updates, or validation performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On the Reliability of Sampling Strategies",
      "link": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3748086",
      "contributions": [
        "The supplied search summary reports that candidate sampling affects model comparisons and agreement with full evaluation.",
        "Motivates separating pinned sampled-candidate validation from full-catalog simulator observations."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not justify changing the host’s fixed evaluator.",
        "Does not establish that healthy training margins imply strong validation HR@10 for these models."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Stratified allocation results",
      "link": "https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/12-001-x/2015002/article/14249/02-eng.htm",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides the stratified-sampling basis for distinguishing allocation from fixed estimator weights.",
        "Motivates the gradient-estimator deduction that fixed-count covariance-trace minimization allocates samples proportional to absolute branch weight times the square root of gradient covariance trace."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The gradient-allocation formula is an adaptation requiring independent sampling, positive fixed counts, and a well-defined within-branch estimand.",
        "Does not establish measured gradient covariances, an optimal terminal probability, or optimal Adam training trajectories in this task.",
        "Random branch absence and target-normalized objectives require additional accounting."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Stochastic Optimization with Importance Sampling",
      "link": "https://proceedings.mlr.press/v37/zhaoa15.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides a basis for importance-weighted stochastic gradients when sampling probabilities differ from desired objective weights.",
        "Helps distinguish an allocation experiment from an objective-reweighting experiment."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Adding importance or presence correction would change the proposed unchanged-reduction intervention.",
        "Unbiased raw gradient estimates do not guarantee improved HR@10 or identical adaptive-optimizer behavior."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Does It Look Sequential? An Analysis of Datasets for Evaluation of Sequential Recommendations",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12008",
      "contributions": [
        "Studies frozen SASRec and GRU4Rec under history shuffling using ranking degradation and recommendation-set overlap.",
        "Motivates controlled BERT4Rec sensitivity probes that keep targets, candidates, padding, and appended MASK fixed."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its findings do not directly establish BERT4Rec behavior or sequential structure in the four requested categories.",
        "Latest-item-preserving shuffles, last-item-only inputs, and matched deletion controls are proposed adaptations.",
        "Sensitivity alone does not establish useful sequence reasoning, causal importance, current intent, or shortcut learning."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
